Task 1: Configure the IdM Certificate Authority
Subtask 1.1: Verify IdM CA Installation Status
First, let's check if the Certificate Authority is properly installed and running within your IdM environment.

Connect to your IdM server using the provided credentials

Check the CA installation status:

sudo ipa-ca-install --help
Verify CA services are running:
sudo systemctl status pki-tomcatd@pki-tomcat.service
sudo systemctl status ipa.service
Check CA certificate information:
sudo ipa ca-show ipa
Subtask 1.2: Configure CA Settings
View current CA configuration:
sudo ipa config-show | grep -i ca
Check certificate profiles available:
sudo ipa certprofile-find
View the default certificate profile:
sudo ipa certprofile-show caIPAserviceCert
Subtask 1.3: Verify CA Certificate Chain
Display the CA certificate:
sudo openssl x509 -in /etc/ipa/ca.crt -text -noout
Check certificate validity:
sudo openssl x509 -in /etc/ipa/ca.crt -noout -dates
Verify certificate chain:
sudo ipa cert-show 1
Task 2: Issue and Manage Certificates Using ipa cert-request
Subtask 2.1: Create a Service Principal for Certificate Request
Create a new service principal:
sudo ipa service-add HTTP/webserver.example.com
Verify the service was created:
sudo ipa service-show HTTP/webserver.example.com
Subtask 2.2: Generate a Private Key and Certificate Signing Request (CSR)
Create a directory for certificate files:
mkdir ~/certificates
cd ~/certificates
Generate a private key:
openssl genrsa -out webserver.key 2048
Create a certificate signing request:
openssl req -new -key webserver.key -out webserver.csr -subj "/CN=webserver.example.com/O=Example Organization/C=US"
Verify the CSR content:
openssl req -in webserver.csr -text -noout
Subtask 2.3: Request Certificate from IdM CA
Submit certificate request to IdM CA:
sudo ipa cert-request webserver.csr --principal=HTTP/webserver.example.com
Note the certificate serial number from the output (you'll need this for management)

Retrieve the issued certificate:

sudo ipa cert-show <SERIAL_NUMBER> --out=webserver.crt
Replace <SERIAL_NUMBER> with the actual serial number from step 1.

Subtask 2.4: Verify the Issued Certificate
Check certificate details:
openssl x509 -in webserver.crt -text -noout
Verify certificate against CA:
openssl verify -CAfile /etc/ipa/ca.crt webserver.crt
Check certificate expiration:
openssl x509 -in webserver.crt -noout -dates
Subtask 2.5: Manage Certificate Lifecycle
List all certificates:
sudo ipa cert-find
Search for certificates by service:
sudo ipa cert-find --service=HTTP/webserver.example.com
Put certificate on hold (revoke temporarily):
sudo ipa cert-revoke <SERIAL_NUMBER> --revocation-reason=6
Remove hold from certificate:
sudo ipa cert-remove-hold <SERIAL_NUMBER>
Task 3: Test Certificate-Based Authentication
Subtask 3.1: Configure Apache Web Server for SSL
Install Apache web server:
sudo dnf install httpd mod_ssl -y
Create SSL configuration directory:
sudo mkdir -p /etc/httpd/ssl
Copy certificate files to Apache directory:
sudo cp ~/certificates/webserver.crt /etc/httpd/ssl/
sudo cp ~/certificates/webserver.key /etc/httpd/ssl/
sudo cp /etc/ipa/ca.crt /etc/httpd/ssl/
Set proper permissions:
sudo chmod 600 /etc/httpd/ssl/webserver.key
sudo chmod 644 /etc/httpd/ssl/webserver.crt
sudo chmod 644 /etc/httpd/ssl/ca.crt
Subtask 3.2: Configure SSL Virtual Host
Create SSL configuration file:
sudo tee /etc/httpd/conf.d/ssl-webserver.conf << 'EOF'
<VirtualHost *:443>
    ServerName webserver.example.com
    DocumentRoot /var/www/html
    
    SSLEngine on
    SSLCertificateFile /etc/httpd/ssl/webserver.crt
    SSLCertificateKeyFile /etc/httpd/ssl/webserver.key
    SSLCACertificateFile /etc/httpd/ssl/ca.crt
    
    SSLProtocol all -SSLv3 -TLSv1 -TLSv1.1
    SSLCipherSuite ECDHE-ECDSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384:ECDHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384
    
    LogLevel warn
    ErrorLog logs/ssl_error_log
    TransferLog logs/ssl_access_log
</VirtualHost>
EOF
Create a test web page:
sudo tee /var/www/html/index.html << 'EOF'
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Certificate Test Page</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>SSL Certificate Test</h1>
    <p>If you can see this page over HTTPS, your certificate is working correctly!</p>
    <p>Server: webserver.example.com</p>
</body>
</html>
EOF
Subtask 3.3: Start and Test Web Server
Start Apache service:
sudo systemctl start httpd
sudo systemctl enable httpd
Check service status:
sudo systemctl status httpd
Open firewall ports:
sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=http
sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=https
sudo firewall-cmd --reload
Subtask 3.4: Test Certificate-Based Authentication
Add hostname to /etc/hosts (for testing purposes):
echo "127.0.0.1 webserver.example.com" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts
Test HTTPS connection with curl:
curl -k https://webserver.example.com
Test certificate validation:
curl --cacert /etc/ipa/ca.crt https://webserver.example.com
Check certificate details via OpenSSL:
echo | openssl s_client -connect webserver.example.com:443 -servername webserver.example.com 2>/dev/null | openssl x509 -noout -text
Subtask 3.5: Test Certificate Revocation
Revoke the certificate:
sudo ipa cert-revoke <SERIAL_NUMBER> --revocation-reason=1
Check certificate status:
sudo ipa cert-show <SERIAL_NUMBER>
Test that revoked certificate is no longer valid:
curl --cacert /etc/ipa/ca.crt https://webserver.example.com
The connection should fail or show certificate errors.

Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Certificate Request Fails
Problem: ipa cert-request command fails with permission errors.

Solution:

# Ensure you have proper Kerberos ticket
kinit admin
# Verify service principal exists
sudo ipa service-show HTTP/webserver.example.com
Issue 2: Apache Won't Start with SSL
Problem: Apache fails to start after SSL configuration.

Solution:

# Check Apache configuration syntax
sudo httpd -t
# Check SSL certificate files permissions
ls -la /etc/httpd/ssl/
# Review error logs
sudo tail -f /var/log/httpd/error_log
Issue 3: Certificate Validation Fails
Problem: Browser or curl shows certificate validation errors.

Solution:

# Verify certificate chain
openssl verify -CAfile /etc/ipa/ca.crt /etc/httpd/ssl/webserver.crt
# Check certificate dates
openssl x509 -in /etc/httpd/ssl/webserver.crt -noout -dates
# Ensure hostname matches certificate CN
openssl x509 -in /etc/httpd/ssl/webserver.crt -noout -subject
Key Concepts Summary
Certificate Authority (CA): A trusted entity that issues digital certificates
Certificate Signing Request (CSR): A request sent to a CA to obtain a digital certificate
Public Key Infrastructure (PKI): Framework for managing digital certificates and public-key encryption
Certificate Revocation: Process of invalidating a certificate before its expiration date
SSL/TLS: Protocols that provide secure communication over networks
